{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\user\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Microsoft\\\\WindowsApps\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -LiteralPath 'C:\\\\Projects\\\\Agent_Workspace\\\\HG-KSEOS\\\\var\\\\swof-construction-002-w2\\\\specs\\\\W2_WO-SWOF-W2-003_SPEC.md' -Raw\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\user\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Microsoft\\\\WindowsApps\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -LiteralPath 'C:\\\\Projects\\\\Agent_Workspace\\\\HG-KSEOS\\\\var\\\\swof-construction-002-w2\\\\specs\\\\W2_WO-SWOF-W2-003_SPEC.md' -Raw\"","aggregated_output":"# WO-SWOF-W2-003 SPEC — SWOF assurance / evidence / SoD substrate + journal-integrity envelope\n\nYou are the tracked mutation writer for SWOF WorkOrder `WO-SWOF-W2-003`.\nYou may write ONLY the files listed in section 1.\n\nHard rules:\n- Python 3.11+, standard library ONLY. ASCII only. LF endings.\n- This is the substrate that decides PASS/FAIL. A checker or oracle that CANNOT fail is the defect\n  this WorkOrder exists to prevent: every rejection class must be injected by a test and must raise\n  its own typed error. An assertion that is initialised and never reached is a vacuous checker.\n- Deterministic: no wall-clock in a decision, no randomness, stable ordering everywhere.\n- Never `except: pass`; per-item failures surface.\n- The journal envelope is SWOF-OWNED and READ-ONLY with respect to HG-KSEOS: it verifies an EXPORTED\n  event stream and never writes to, or claims cryptographic protection of, the HGK database.\n\n## 1. Exact file list\n\n```\nsrc/assurance/__init__.py\nsrc/assurance/README.md\nsrc/assurance/predicate.py\nsrc/assurance/oracle.py\nsrc/assurance/evidence.py\nsrc/assurance/sod.py\nsrc/assurance/journal.py\nsrc/assurance/tests/__init__.py\nsrc/assurance/tests/test_predicate.py\nsrc/assurance/tests/test_oracle.py\nsrc/assurance/tests/test_evidence.py\nsrc/assurance/tests/test_sod.py\nsrc/assurance/tests/test_journal.py\nsrc/assurance/tests/test_adversarial.py\nschemas/assurance/README.md\nschemas/assurance/acceptance_predicate.schema.json\nschemas/assurance/evidence_plan.schema.json\n```\n\nTests use the repository convention (`ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3]`, then\n`sys.path.insert(0, str(ROOT / \"src\"))`, then `from assurance.xxx import ...`). Every test is inside a\n`unittest.TestCase`; the module ends with `if __name__ == \"__main__\": unittest.main()`.\n\n## 2. Required API and behaviour\n\n### 2.1 `src/assurance/predicate.py`\n\n```python\nclass PredicateInvalid(Exception): code = \"ERR_PREDICATE_INVALID\"\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass AcceptancePredicate:\n    predicate_id: str\n    subject_id: str\n    subject_type: str            # DELIVERABLE | CAPABILITY | JOURNEY | REQUIREMENT\n    required_evidence_kinds: frozenset[str]\n    oracle_id: str\n    terminal_states: tuple[str, ...]\n    negative_fixtures: tuple[str, ...]\n    raw_evidence_required: bool = True\n    independent_checker_required: bool = True\n\ndef validate(predicate) -> AcceptancePredicate\ndef digest(predicate) -> str\n```\n\nLaws (raise `PredicateInvalid` with a specific `reason_code` attribute):\n1. empty `oracle_id`, empty `required_evidence_kinds`, empty `negative_fixtures`, an unknown\n   `subject_type` or `terminal_states` with fewer than two states are all invalid — a predicate that\n   cannot fail is not a predicate.\n2. `validate` must validate the RAW mapping/object BEFORE constructing anything, so a malformed input\n   raises `PredicateInvalid` rather than a generic `TypeError`.\n3. `digest` covers immutable semantic content only (not runtime binding state); two predicates that\n   differ only in a runtime field cannot collide, and the same predicate always digests the same.\n\n### 2.2 `src/assurance/oracle.py`\n\n```python\nclass OracleAmbiguous(Exception):  code = \"ERR_ORACLE_AMBIGUOUS\"\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass OracleVerdict:\n    passed: bool\n    first_failure: str | None\n    checked: tuple[str, ...]\n    failures: tuple[str, ...]\n    reason_code: str\n\nclass Oracle:\n    def __init__(self, oracle_id: str): ...\n    def required_kinds(self) -> frozenset[str]\n    def decide(self, predicate, items) -> OracleVerdict\n```\n\nLaws:\n1. FIRST-FAILURE semantics: `decide` evaluates in a stable, documented order and stops at the first\n   failing condition; `first_failure` names it. Evaluation must not continue to collect \"nicer\"\n   failures after the first one in a way that changes the verdict.\n2. an oracle whose `oracle_id` has no rules raises `OracleAmbiguous` (an oracle must be defined).\n3. missing required evidence kind, a duplicate evidence kind, an item whose `subject_id` differs from\n   the predicate subject, or a non-deterministic item ordering each produce a distinct `reason_code`.\n4. `decide` NEVER returns `passed=True` when any `negative_fixtures` entry is unrepresented in the\n   checked set: an untested negative fixture means the predicate is not yet decidable.\n5. the verdict is a pure function of (predicate, items): identical inputs produce identical verdicts,\n   including the order of `checked` and `failures`.\n\n### 2.3 `src/assurance/evidence.py`\n\n```python\nLINKAGE_MODES = (\"DIRECT_FINAL_SUBJECT\", \"TRANSITIVE_PRE_MUTATION_LINEAGE\",\n                 \"COMPILER_CONTRACT_BOUND\", \"WORKORDER_CHECKPOINT\")\n\nREASONS = (\"STALE\", \"FOREIGN\", \"WRONG_SUBJECT\", \"SELF_REFERENTIAL\", \"FORGED_SUMMARY\",\n           \"DIRECT_FINAL_CLAIM_ON_PRE_MUTATION\", \"BRANCH_TIP_SUBSTITUTION\", \"HASH_MISMATCH\",\n           \"RAW_PROOF_MISSING\", \"CHECKER_EQUALS_PRODUCER\")\n\nclass EvidenceRejected(Exception):\n    code = \"ERR_EVIDENCE_REJECTED\"\n    # instances carry .reason_code (one of REASONS) and .item_id\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass EvidenceItem:\n    item_id: str; kind: str; subject_id: str; subject_version: str; sha256: str\n    environment: str; action: str; checker_id: str; producer_id: str\n    raw_path: str | None; linkage_mode: str; is_pre_mutation: bool = False\n    content: bytes | None = None; declared_subject_id: str | None = None\n    is_branch_tip: bool = False; is_summary_only: bool = False; stale: bool = False\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass EvidencePlan:\n    plan_id: str; subject_id: str; subject_sha: str; oracle_id: str\n    required: tuple[tuple[str, str, str], ...]   # (kind, linkage_mode, environment)\n    checker_id: str; producer_id: str\n\ndef validate_item(item, *, plan, resolver=None) -> dict\ndef validate_plan(plan, items, *, resolver=None) -> dict\ndef invalidate(plan, *, changed_identity, gates) -> list[dict]\n```\n\n`resolver` is an injected callable `(path) -> bytes | None` used to hash RAW artifacts; the tests pass\na dict-backed resolver. No filesystem access at import time.\n\nLaws (each injectable as a test):\n1. a missing or mismatching `sha256` against the resolved raw bytes raises `HASH_MISMATCH`.\n2. `is_summary_only=True` with no `raw_path` raises `FORGED_SUMMARY` — a summary boolean is not\n   evidence.\n3. `subject_id` differing from the plan subject raises `WRONG_SUBJECT`; a `declared_subject_id`\n   differing from `subject_id` also raises `WRONG_SUBJECT` (hash integrity proves transport, not\n   meaning).\n4. `is_pre_mutation=True` with `linkage_mode=\"DIRECT_FINAL_SUBJECT\"` raises\n   `DIRECT_FINAL_CLAIM_ON_PRE_MUTATION`; a pre-mutation artifact must declare\n   `TRANSITIVE_PRE_MUTATION_LINEAGE`.\n5. `is_branch_tip=True` raises `BRANCH_TIP_SUBSTITUTION` when the plan requires an immutable SHA.\n6. `stale=True` raises `STALE`; an item whose `environment`/`action` is not the planned one raises\n   `FOREIGN`.\n7. `checker_id == producer_id` raises `CHECKER_EQUALS_PRODUCER` (also see `sod.py`).\n8. a self-referential item (its `raw_path` names the pack that contains it, detectable from the plan)\n   raises `SELF_REFERENTIAL`.\n9. `validate_plan` returns\n   `{\"schema\": \"SWOF-EVIDENCE-PLAN-VERDICT/1\", \"passed\": bool, \"checked\": int, \"rejected\": [...],\n     \"linkage_modes\": {...per-mode counts...}, \"required_present\": int, \"invalid_direct_link_count\": int,\n     \"invalid_transitive_link_count\": int}` and the per-mode counters are SEPARATE (one mixed counter\n     both over- and under-reports).\n10. `invalidate` returns the gates whose evidence is invalidated by a changed identity (subject SHA,\n    oracle id, environment, or security-relevant config) — the invalidation cone.\n\n### 2.4 `src/assurance/sod.py`\n\n```python\nclass SoDViolation(Exception):        code = \"ERR_SOD_VIOLATION\"\nclass CheckerWriteRefused(Exception): code = \"ERR_CHECKER_WRITE_REFUSED\"\n\ndef assert_separated(producer_id, checker_id, *, independent=True) -> dict\nclass ReadOnlyChecker:\n    def __init__(self, checker_id: str, *, allowed_evidence_root: str = \"\"): ...\n    @property\n    def identity(self) -> str\n    def read(self, path) -> None          # allowed: reading the frozen candidate\n    def write_product(self, path, content) -> None   # ALWAYS raises CheckerWriteRefused\n    def write_evidence(self, path, content) -> dict  # allowed only inside allowed_evidence_root\n    def record(self) -> dict\n```\n\nLaws:\n1. `assert_separated` raises `SoDViolation` when the identities are equal or empty, and returns a\n   receipt `{\"schema\": \"SWOF-SOD-RECEIPT/1\", \"producer\": ..., \"checker\": ..., \"separated\": True,\n   \"independent\": ...}`.\n2. `ReadOnlyChecker.write_product` ALWAYS raises `CheckerWriteRefused`; a checker may not repair the\n   candidate, its evaluator logic, the WorkOrder or the evidence contract.\n3. `write_evidence` outside the allowed evidence root raises `CheckerWriteRefused`.\n4. the checker records every read and every refused write in its receipt so the enforcement is\n   demonstrable rather than asserted.\n\n### 2.5 `src/assurance/journal.py` — W2-JOURNAL-INTEGRITY-ASSESSMENT implementation\n\n```python\nclass JournalTamperDetected(Exception): code = \"ERR_JOURNAL_TAMPER_DETECTED\"\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass JournalEntry:\n    seq: int; event_id: str; payload: dict; prev_digest: str; entry_digest: str\n\nclass DigestChainJournal:\n    def __init__(self, chain_id: str, *, genesis_digest=None): ...\n    def append(self, *, event_id, payload) -> JournalEntry\n    def verify(self) -> dict      # {\"schema\": ..., \"intact\": bool, \"checked\": int,\n                                  #  \"reason_code\": ..., \"first_broken_seq\": int | None}\n    def export_manifest(self) -> dict\n    @staticmethod\n    def verify_manifest(manifest, entries) -> dict\n\ndef from_lifecycle_rows(rows, *, chain_id=\"HGK-LIFECYCLE-EXPORT\") -> DigestChainJournal\ndef threat_assessment() -> dict\n```\n\nLaws:\n1. `entry_digest` is `sha256(chain_id | seq | event_id | prev_digest | canonical_json(payload))`; the\n   chain is bound to `chain_id`, so an entry from another chain cannot be spliced in.\n2. `verify` detects, with distinct `reason_code`s: payload mutation (`ENTRY_DIGEST_MISMATCH`),\n   removal/truncation (`SEQ_GAP`), reordering (`PREV_DIGEST_MISMATCH`), foreign chain\n   (`CHAIN_ID_MISMATCH`) and header tampering (`HEADER_MISMATCH`). An intact chain returns\n   `intact=True`, `checked=len(entries)`, `first_broken_seq=None`.\n3. `export_manifest` returns the chain id, the final digest, the entry count and a per-entry digest\n   list; `verify_manifest` re-checks a manifest against a re-exported entry list.\n4. `from_lifecycle_rows` accepts the raw `[{rowid, transition_id, from_state, to_state, created_at}]`\n   rows read from the HGK lifecycle stream and builds a chain WITHOUT writing anything back.\n5. `threat_assessment()` returns a dict stating, explicitly: the envelope gives DETECTION of\n   post-export modification, it does NOT make the HGK database tamper-proof, it is NOT a cryptographic\n   signature (no key management), it is read-only with respect to HG-KSEOS, and it should NOT be\n   backported into the accepted W1 subject for evidence aesthetics. Include\n   `{\"schema\": \"SWOF-W2-JOURNAL-INTEGRITY-ASSESSMENT/1\",\n     \"implemented\": True, \"protection_class\": \"DETECTION_ONLY_NO_KEY_MANAGEMENT\",\n     \"db_level_enforcement_claimed\": False, \"writes_to_hgk\": False, \"justified\": True}`.\n\n## 3. Schemas\n\n- `schemas/assurance/acceptance_predicate.schema.json`: `additionalProperties: false`, required\n  `predicate_id/subject_id/subject_type/required_evidence_kinds/oracle_id/terminal_states/`\n  `negative_fixtures`, `subject_type` enum, `terminal_states` minItems 2.\n- `schemas/assurance/evidence_plan.schema.json`: required `plan_id/subject_id/subject_sha/oracle_id/`\n  `required/checker_id/producer_id`, `required` items are 3-element arrays of strings,\n  `additionalProperties: false`.\n- `schemas/assurance/README.md` documents the predicate/oracle/plan/SoD/journal contract and the\n  explicit non-claim that this is not a signature and not DB-level enforcement.\n\n## 4. Required adversarial tests (at least 40 across the files)\n\nEach asserts its exact typed error / `reason_code`:\n1. forged summary without raw proof -> `FORGED_SUMMARY`.\n2. wrong SHA with plausible content -> `HASH_MISMATCH`.\n3. maker-as-checker -> `CHECKER_EQUALS_PRODUCER` and `SoDViolation`.\n4. checker mutation attempt -> `CheckerWriteRefused` from `write_product`, and the candidate file is\n   unchanged afterwards.\n5. evidence tamper -> mutate one journal payload and assert `JournalTamperDetected` /\n   `ENTRY_DIGEST_MISMATCH` from `verify`; delete an entry and assert `SEQ_GAP`.\n6. stale pre-mutation object falsely marked direct-final -> `DIRECT_FINAL_CLAIM_ON_PRE_MUTATION`.\n7. branch-tip substitution -> `BRANCH_TIP_SUBSTITUTION`.\n8. an oracle that cannot fail must be impossible: an empty predicate raises `PredicateInvalid`, an\n   oracle with no rules raises `OracleAmbiguous`, and a predicate whose negative fixtures are not\n   represented in the checked set does NOT pass.\n9. `validate_plan` reports partial-failure counters separately and never passes on an empty item list.\n\n## 5. Definition of done\n\n`python -m unittest discover -s src/assurance/tests -t src/assurance/tests` reports OK with the full\ncount. Do not modify any path outside `src/assurance/**` and `schemas/assurance/**`.\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
